  "reviewer_notes": "The paper is a case study of two Romanian industrial end-users (aluminium/metal processing and cement) evaluating a green-certificate cost-exemption scheme. Energy performance is captured with two intensity indicators — specific electricity use (SEC, Eq. 1) and Energy Intensity (EI, Eq. 2) — applied per end-user; these were modelled as four models (M1-M4). A green-certificate cost calculation (Eq. 3) is financial rather than an energy performance model and was not extracted as a model. K1 (price distorting EI) is strongly demonstrated and directly motivates the weighted-average-price adaptation (D1). K2 and K3 are borderline/requires inference: K2 concerns the electricity-only indicator not capturing cement's dominant fossil-fuel energy, and K3 concerns seasonal construction demand driving electricity use; both are framed around the case rather than proven statistically. No explicit study limitation is stated in the paper (future-work statements were not coded).",
